winex11: Don't start a pointer move or resize with no button down.

move_resize_window() asks the window manager to start the move with
_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE, giving the button that is down. When no button is down it
still sent the request, with button 0 and a pointer direction, and returned.
Per EWMH, button 0 is for a keyboard move (directions 9 and 10); a pointer
direction with it is not well formed, and a window manager that honors it starts
a move that nothing ends: it waits for a button release that already happened,
so the window follows the pointer until the next click.

An application gets there by running DefWindowProc for the caption click again
after the first one ended its move: the ROTK Launcher (Electron/Chromium) does,
and the second run reaches X11DRV_SysCommand with the button already up.

Real Windows returns from SC_MOVE|HTCAPTION at once when no button is down
(measured on Windows 10 build 19045 by calling SendMessage(WM_SYSCOMMAND,
SC_MOVE|HTCAPTION) with the mouse idle: it returns in 0 ms and does not enter
the move loop; the same call with a keyboard SC_MOVE blocks until Escape).
Do the same here for the pointer directions. Keyboard moves are unchanged.

---
--- a/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
+++ b/dlls/winex11.drv/mouse.c
@@ -1523,6 +1523,17 @@

     TRACE( "hwnd %p/%lx, pos %s, dir %d, button %d\n", hwnd, win, wine_dbgstr_point(&pos), dir, button );

+    /* Directions 0-8 are for a pointer, 9 (SIZE_KEYBOARD) and 10 (MOVE_KEYBOARD) for the keyboard.
+     * A pointer move or resize needs a button held: Windows returns from the system command at once
+     * when none is, which happens when an application runs DefWindowProc again after its drag has
+     * ended. A window manager asked for a pointer move with button 0 starts one that nothing ends,
+     * as it is waiting for a button release that already happened. */
+    if (!button && dir < 9)
+    {
+        TRACE( "no button is down, not starting a pointer move or resize\n" );
+        return;
+    }
+
     xev.xclient.type = ClientMessage;
     xev.xclient.window = win;
     xev.xclient.message_type = x11drv_atom(_NET_WM_MOVERESIZE);
